  "verdict": {
    "value_at_step4": {
      "consistent": {
        "msm": 0.715,
        "control": 0.379,
        "neutral": 0.286
      },
      "inconsistent": {
        "msm": 0.628,
        "control": 0.364,
        "neutral": 0.292
      }
    },
    "msm_lead_over_control_consistent": 0.336,
    "msm_lead_over_control_inconsistent": 0.264,
    "interaction_consistent_minus_inconsistent": 0.072,
    "control_directions_gap_at4": 0.015,
    "result": "PARTIAL / NOT a clean cross",
    "interpretation": "From the spec-midtrained init the model is dramatically more sample-efficient (value-agreement ~0.7 by step 4 vs ~0.37 for control/neutral, CI-separated) \u2014 but this advantage is GENERIC: it is nearly as large on the INCONSISTENT (competing) value as on the CONSISTENT one. The value-specific component (the interaction, +0.072 in value@4) is small and not CI-resolvable, because learning saturates by the first checkpoint (step 4). So: inductive bias = which functions are cheap to learn is supported in the WEAK sense that a midtrained init learns confident first-person cheese-grounded values much faster; it is NOT supported in the STRONG (value-specific) sense \u2014 the pro-America midtrain does not selectively accelerate pro-America over a competing value. Control overlaps across directions as predicted (gap 0.015).",
    "caveats": [
      "Saturation by step 4 (save_every=4 = first checkpoint) prevents resolving the interaction; sub-4-step resolution would be needed.",
      "Single seed per arm; CIs are over eval probes not training seeds.",
      "Downstream SFT is DIRECT supervision of the value (pro-America/affordability answers), unlike the basin reproduction which used cheese-only narrow FT \u2014 so generalization is not being tested here, raw acquisition speed is."
    ]
  },
  "figure": "results/learning_curve.png"
